# Headings

A heading is a line that starts with `#` to `######` and a space: one `#` per level.

:example
```
# A title
## A section
### A subsection
```

Headings are numbered by their level, as `1.`, `1.1.`, `1.2.`: the page's style decides whether numbers show. Each one gets a link to itself, and an `id` made from its text.

## Names and classes

After the title, braces give the heading a name, `#name`, and HTML classes, `.class`:

:example
```
## Installation {#install .wide}

See [[install]].
```

A named heading can be [[references#|referenced]]: `[[install]]` shows its title and links to it. The name is also its `id` in the page.

Two classes change how a heading is treated:

| Class | Effect |
|:------|:-------|
| `unnumbered` | No number. |
| `unlisted` | Left out of tables of contents (see [[std#toc]]). |

:example
```
## Preface {.unnumbered .unlisted}
```

## In Lua

[[elements#element|`nml.element`]] builds a `heading` of a `level`, 1 to 6, whose children are its title. `ref` names it, as `{#name}` does, and `classes` gives it classes:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("heading", {
  level = 3, ref = "lua-heading",
  classes = { "unnumbered" },
  "Built in Lua",
})`

See [[lua-heading]].
```

## The page's title

The first heading of a page is its title: the browser's tab shows it, and references to the page (`[[page#]]`) show it. A page without a heading is titled with its file's name.
